Straight-line by design: this program initialises one private scheduler block,
one task block, spawns once, reads two words and prints — no loop, confirmed
by inspection. There is nothing to root either — every block it touches is
untraced module state (§11.11), never a GC object — so a precise-rooting
oracle has no object here to be wrong about.

`BIT_GC=stress` does not report zero, though: 5/5 runs measured
`collections=101`, matching stress.bit's own documented scheduler-loop floor
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`, which every worker OS thread
keeps running for the life of the process regardless of what this program
does). None of those 101 fire at a back-edge this program emitted, and none of
them touch a GC object, so the collection count still verifies nothing here.

Adding a loop to earn the oracle would also destroy the property: the whole
assertion is that the live worker's `dqTail` did not move ACROSS THE SPAWN, and
a collection between the two samples could resume `main` on a different worker
and invalidate the comparison.
